# Evaluating expressions and blocks.
# Origin: REBOL/Core User Guide 2.3, sections 4.1 to 4.7.

--- id evaluation/console-addition
--- origin REBOL/Core User Guide 2.3 section 4.1
--- requires op
--- note The very first example in the guide, and it needs infix.
--- code
1 + 2
--- result
3

--- id evaluation/do-block
--- origin REBOL/Core User Guide 2.3 section 4.3
--- requires op
--- code
do [1 + 2]
--- result
3

--- id evaluation/do-block-yields-last-expression
--- origin REBOL/Core User Guide 2.3 section 4.3
--- requires op
--- note Pins that a block is a sequence of expressions and its value is
--- note the last one, not a collection of all of them.
--- code
do [
    1 + 2
    3 + 4
]
--- result
7

--- id evaluation/do-block-prefix-only
--- origin adapted from REBOL/Core User Guide 2.3 section 4.3
--- note The same behaviour written without infix, so milestone 1 can run
--- note it. If this passes and do-block does not, the gap is op! alone.
--- code
do [
    add 1 2
    add 3 4
]
--- result
7

--- id evaluation/nested-prefix-calls
--- origin adapted from REBOL/Core User Guide 2.3 section 4.1
--- note Argument gathering is itself a full evaluation, so the first
--- note add's second argument is what the second add produced.
--- code
add 1 add 2 3
--- result
6

--- id evaluation/empty-block-is-unset
--- origin REBOL/Core User Guide 2.3 section 4.3
--- note Not none. UNSET is what you get from something that returned
--- note nothing; NONE is a value somebody chose.
--- code
unset? do []
--- result
true

--- id evaluation/do-string
--- origin REBOL/Core User Guide 2.3 section 4.6
--- requires op
--- note DO of a string loads then evaluates, so this exercises the
--- note reader and the evaluator together.
--- code
do "1 + 2"
--- result
3

--- id evaluation/reduce-block
--- origin REBOL/Core User Guide 2.3 section 4.4
--- requires op series
--- note REDUCE evaluates every expression and collects the results,
--- note which is the contrast case to DO returning only the last.
--- code
reduce [
    1 + 2
    3 + 4
]
--- result
[3 7]

--- id evaluation/paren-forces-grouping
--- origin adapted from REBOL/Core User Guide 2.3 section 4.4
--- note There is no operator precedence in REBOL, so a paren is the only
--- note way to change grouping.
--- code
add 1 multiply 2 3
--- result
7

--- id evaluation/paren-evaluates-inline
--- origin adapted from REBOL/Core User Guide 2.3 section 4.4
--- code
add (add 1 2) 4
--- result
7

--- id evaluation/divide-by-zero
--- origin REBOL/Core User Guide 2.3 section 4.7
--- requires op
--- note Asserted by category and id, not by message text. R2 and R3 word
--- note this differently and the wording is not the behaviour.
--- code
100 / 0
--- error math zero-divide

--- id evaluation/divide-by-zero-prefix
--- origin adapted from REBOL/Core User Guide 2.3 section 4.7
--- code
divide 100 0
--- error math zero-divide

--- id evaluation/unset-word-in-expression
--- origin REBOL/Core User Guide 2.3 section 4.7
--- requires op
--- code
size + 10
--- error script no-value

--- id evaluation/wrong-argument-type
--- origin REBOL/Core User Guide 2.3 section 4.7
--- requires op
--- note The guide reports "Cannot use add on block! value". JEBOL raises
--- note expect-arg from the same cause.
--- code
10 + [size]
--- error script expect-arg

--- id evaluation/wrong-argument-type-prefix
--- origin adapted from REBOL/Core User Guide 2.3 section 4.7
--- code
add 10 [size]
--- error script expect-arg

--- id evaluation/decimal-division-precision
--- origin REBOL/Core User Guide 2.3 section 10, confirmed against R3 3.22.1
--- note Fifteen significant digits, not the shortest form that reads back.
--- note This repo asserted the shortest form for a while, on the reasoning
--- note that R3 must differ from R2 here. Running a real R3 says otherwise:
--- note mold 10 / 3 gives 3.33333333333333 and mold 0.1 + 0.2 gives 0.3.
--- note The guide was right and the reasoning was wrong.
--- code
divide 10 3
--- result
3.33333333333333

--- id evaluation/no-operator-precedence
--- origin REBOL 3 Concepts: Math: Evaluation Order
--- requires op
--- note The single most consequential fact about REBOL arithmetic. All
--- note operators bind equally and expressions run strictly left to right,
--- note so this is 20 and not 14. Nothing to remember except left to right.
--- code
2 + 3 * 4
--- result
20

--- id evaluation/operator-chain-runs-left-to-right
--- origin REBOL 3 Concepts: Math: Evaluation Order
--- requires op
--- code
1 + 2 + 3 + 4
--- result
10

--- id evaluation/operator-reaches-into-a-prefix-argument
--- origin adapted from REBOL/Core User Guide 2.3 section 4.1
--- note The counterpart to the entry above, and the reason the rule is
--- note subtler than it looks. An operator takes a single value, so the
--- note addition in 2 + 3 * 4 finishes first. A prefix function takes a
--- note whole expression, so the multiplication here finishes inside the
--- note second argument and this is 7, not 9.
--- code
add 1 2 * 3
--- result
7

--- id evaluation/paren-overrides-the-left-to-right-rule
--- origin adapted from REBOL/Core User Guide 2.3 section 4.4
--- note With no precedence to override, a paren is the only way to group.
--- code
2 + (3 * 4)
--- result
14

--- id evaluation/decimals-mold-to-fifteen-digits
--- origin confirmed against R3 3.22.1 on this machine
--- note The rule, pinned by running one: fifteen significant digits, always
--- note a decimal point, lowercase e with no plus for the exponent.
--- code
reduce [mold 0.1 + 0.2 mold 1.0 mold 100.0 mold divide 1 3]
--- result
["0.3" "1.0" "100.0" "0.333333333333333"]

--- id evaluation/a-decimal-always-keeps-its-point
--- origin confirmed against R3 3.22.1
--- note So a decimal reads back as a decimal rather than as an integer.
--- code
mold 1.0
--- result
"1.0"
